# High-Performance HTTP Proxy with Adaptive Caching

A systems-focused project inspired by classic cache and proxy assignments, redesigned as a polished engineering portfolio project for performance, systems, and cloud roles.

## Overview

This project models a simplified but realistic HTTP proxy and cache layer. The system supports:

- request forwarding and caching
- LRU and LFU eviction strategies
- workload-aware adaptive policy selection
- multithreaded request processing
- benchmarking and visualization of policy trade-offs
- synthetic load testing for throughput and hit-rate analysis

The result is a compact yet credible systems project that demonstrates performance engineering thinking without being a direct copy of a classroom assignment.

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Client Requests] --> B[Proxy Worker Thread]
    B --> C{Cached?}
    C -- Yes --> D[Serve from cache]
    C -- No --> E[Fetch / simulate backend response]
    E --> F[Store in cache]
    F --> D
    G[Benchmark Harness] --> H[LRU vs LFU Workloads]
    H --> I[Hit-rate / latency comparison]
    I --> J[Results + plots]
```

## Why this project is strong for a resume

This project highlights the engineering skills that matter in systems and infrastructure roles:

- cache policy design and eviction analysis
- concurrency and thread-safe coordination
- benchmarking under realistic workload patterns
- data-driven optimization decisions based on measured results
- thoughtful tradeoffs between latency, hit rate, and memory usage

